  "date": "Tue, 11 Aug 2026 21:29:31 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n☀️ Plus: Spanish sunrise | Tuesday, August 11, 2026\r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n Axios Finish Line By\r\nMike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei\r\n &middot; Aug 11, 2026 \r\nAug 11, 2026\r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 379 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Amy Stern. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n1 big thing: Exercise your brain\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Gabriella Turrisi/Axios\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Your daily crossword is still a flex. But scientists are testing a more focused question: Can you train an aging brain to process information faster — and potentially change its trajectory?\n\n 🧩 Why it matters: A growing body of research suggests certain kinds of cognitive exercises can produce measurable changes in the brain. One long-running trial even linked a specific type of training to fewer dementia diagnoses, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. 💡 The evidence: Recent studies highlighted by The Washington Post ( gift link ) suggest that targeted cognitive training may affect systems involved in attention and memory as well as brain structure.\n\n ⚡ Zoom in: A separate, much smaller randomized clinical trial presented last month at the Alzheimer's Association International Conference offers a clue about what might be happening biologically. \n\n Researchers from Clemson University, the Medical University of South Carolina and Penn State randomly assigned 53 participants to three groups that trained two to three hours a week for about three months. 🔬 One group practiced higher-order thinking, such as planning, reasoning and shifting attention. \n\n Another concentrated on processing speed and visual attention. In one exercise, for example, participants had to identify a vehicle flashed briefly in the center of a screen while also remembering where a Route 66 sign appeared. A third, active-control group played familiar games, including Solitaire, word searches and a version of Connect Four. Before and after the training, researchers took blood samples to measure two forms of amyloid beta, a protein closely associated with Alzheimer's disease. \n\n Only the speed-training group showed favorable shifts in those Alzheimer's-related biomarkers. 🧪 Reality check: The science is still early. Researchers don't yet know whether those changes translate into better everyday functioning or lower disease risk. \n\n There's still no single accepted measure of a \"healthy\" brain, and brain training isn't a substitute for exercise or other healthy habits. The bottom line: Keep up your Sudoku if you enjoy it. \n\n The bigger question now: Can scientists figure out which mental workouts actually help our brains age better? \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA message from Axios\r\n\n\r\n \r\n Don't Miss: Our Quarterly Report on Ad Economics \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive just released its exclusive quarterly report on the forces reshaping ad economics.\n\n\r\n \r\n Built for executives, it pairs expert reporting with essential data and charts to show where ad dollars are moving next. \r\n \r\n 🔒 Become a member today to unlock this exclusive Media Trends Executive report.\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n2. 🌅 Parting shots: San Sebastián shines\r\n \r\n \r\n Photo: Jeff Turner 🌊 Morning blue gives way to golden light over San Sebastián's harbor in Spain's Basque Country. \n\n Thanks to reader Jeff Turner of Little Washington, Va., for sending these along. Photo: Jeff Turner \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n\r\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
