style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #848484;\">\r\n<p>Illustration: Gabriella Turrisi/Axios</p>\r\n</span></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td height=\"15\" style=\"height: 15px; font-size: 0;\">&nbsp;</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td class=\"v\" style=\"padding: 0 3.5%; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 17px; font-family: Georgia, serif; line-height: 1.75; color: #222222; letter-spacing: -0.1px;\">\r\n<p><strong>Your daily crossword</strong> is still a flex. But scientists are testing a more focused question: Can you train an aging brain to process information faster — and potentially change its trajectory?</p><ul><li>🧩 <strong>Why it matters:</strong> A growing body of research suggests certain kinds of cognitive exercises can produce measurable changes in the brain. One long-running trial even linked a specific type of training to fewer dementia diagnoses, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></li></ul><p>💡 <strong>The evidence: </strong>Recent studies highlighted by The Washington Post (<em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47001685.406965/aHR0cHM6Ly93YXBvLnN0LzQ1T0RaTFI_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB0b79159d\" target=\"_blank\">gift link</a></em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47001685.406965/aHR0cHM6Ly93YXBvLnN0LzQ1T0RaTFI_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aC0b79159d\" target=\"_blank\"></a>) suggest that targeted cognitive training may affect systems involved in attention and memory as well as brain structure.</p><p>⚡ <strong>Zoom in: </strong>A separate, much smaller randomized clinical trial presented last month at the Alzheimer's Association International Conference offers a clue about what might be happening biologically. </p><ul><li><strong>Researchers from Clemson University,</strong> the Medical University of South Carolina and Penn State randomly assigned 53 participants to three groups that trained two to three hours a week for about three months.</li></ul><p><strong>🔬 One group practiced</strong> higher-order thinking, such as planning, reasoning and shifting attention. </p><ul><li><strong>Another concentrated on</strong> processing speed and visual attention. In one exercise, for example, participants had to identify a vehicle flashed briefly in the center of a screen while also remembering where a Route 66 sign appeared.</li><li><strong>A third, active-control group</strong> played familiar games, including Solitaire, word searches and a version of Connect Four. </li></ul><p><strong>Before and after the training,</strong> researchers took blood samples to measure two forms of amyloid beta, a protein closely associated with Alzheimer's disease. </p><ul><li><strong>Only the speed-training</strong> group showed favorable shifts in those Alzheimer's-related biomarkers.</li></ul><p>🧪 <strong>Reality check:</strong> The science is still early. Researchers don't yet know whether those changes translate into better everyday functioning or lower disease risk. </p><ul><li><strong>There's still no single</strong> accepted measure of a \"healthy\" brain, and brain<strong> </strong>training isn't a substitute for exercise or other healthy habits. </li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line: </strong>Keep up your Sudoku if you enjoy it. </p><ul><li><strong>The bigger question now:</strong> Can scientists figure out which mental workouts actually help our brains age better?</li></ul>\r\n</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47001685.406965/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtMTQ4MjRjMTctMWQwNi00OGY2LTk2NjYtY2NlZjIzNzc3MWNlLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4